WEBVTT

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:02.365
Lesson 5.7.

00:00:02.365 --> 00:00:06.625
Operating across chains: gas, routes & chain abstraction.

00:00:06.625 --> 00:00:08.574
By the end, you'll be able to move

00:00:08.574 --> 00:00:11.581
value between chains cheaply and safely, without ever

00:00:11.581 --> 00:00:14.700
getting stranded somewhere you can't afford to transact.

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:17.290
Here's the plan.

00:00:17.290 --> 00:00:20.083
Gas stranding, exactly what it is, and why

00:00:20.083 --> 00:00:23.630
it's a genuinely simple problem to avoid entirely.

00:00:23.630 --> 00:00:26.282
The four real route choices for moving value

00:00:26.282 --> 00:00:29.235
between chains, and how they actually differ.

00:00:29.235 --> 00:00:32.254
Chain abstraction, what it hides, and why every

00:00:32.254 --> 00:00:35.070
hidden layer is still a real dependency.

00:00:35.070 --> 00:00:37.384
And a full worked example, moving thirty

00:00:37.384 --> 00:00:40.205
thousand dollars, choosing the route deliberately.

00:00:41.505 --> 00:00:42.505
Gas stranding.

00:00:43.805 --> 00:00:46.793
Here's exactly what gas stranding actually is, and

00:00:46.793 --> 00:00:49.670
why it happens more often than you'd expect.

00:00:49.670 --> 00:00:52.010
You hold real, valuable tokens on a

00:00:52.010 --> 00:00:54.155
chain, but you hold none of that

00:00:54.155 --> 00:00:56.925
specific chain's own native gas token.

00:00:56.925 --> 00:00:59.467
Without it, you genuinely cannot pay for a

00:00:59.467 --> 00:01:02.777
single transaction, meaning you can't move those tokens

00:01:02.777 --> 00:01:05.378
anywhere at all, no matter how much they're

00:01:05.378 --> 00:01:08.393
actually worth, until you somehow get that chain's

00:01:08.393 --> 00:01:10.344
gas token onto that same address.

00:01:11.644 --> 00:01:14.477
Worth being completely direct about the actual

00:01:14.477 --> 00:01:17.068
fix here, since it's genuinely simple, and

00:01:17.068 --> 00:01:19.839
entirely avoidable with almost no effort.

00:01:19.839 --> 00:01:22.334
Keep a small gas float, a modest amount

00:01:22.334 --> 00:01:25.016
of that chain's own native token, on every

00:01:25.016 --> 00:01:27.823
single chain you actually use, at all times,

00:01:27.823 --> 00:01:30.816
specifically before you ever need it, not after

00:01:30.816 --> 00:01:33.249
you've already discovered you're stuck.

00:01:34.549 --> 00:01:36.874
Here's how to actually recover, if you've

00:01:36.874 --> 00:01:40.085
already discovered you're genuinely stranded, rather than

00:01:40.085 --> 00:01:41.991
just prevented it in advance.

00:01:41.991 --> 00:01:44.830
Send a small amount of that specific chain's

00:01:44.830 --> 00:01:47.795
gas token, from an exchange, or another wallet

00:01:47.795 --> 00:01:50.745
you own, directly to the stranded address.

00:01:50.745 --> 00:01:53.739
Some bridges and faucets can also deliver a

00:01:53.739 --> 00:01:57.004
small amount of gas token alongside an incoming

00:01:57.004 --> 00:02:00.570
transfer, specifically for this exact situation.

00:02:00.570 --> 00:02:03.319
And once you've recovered, immediately add that

00:02:03.319 --> 00:02:05.725
chain to your own ongoing gas-float habit.

00:02:07.025 --> 00:02:08.115
Quick check.

00:02:08.115 --> 00:02:09.840
What exactly is gas stranding?

00:02:14.260 --> 00:02:17.037
The answer: having real tokens on a chain,

00:02:17.037 --> 00:02:19.427
but no gas token to actually pay for

00:02:19.427 --> 00:02:22.850
a transaction, meaning you genuinely can't move them.

00:02:24.150 --> 00:02:25.615
Four real route choices.

00:02:26.915 --> 00:02:29.917
Here's all four ways to actually move value between

00:02:29.917 --> 00:02:32.930
chains, and what each one genuinely trades off.

00:02:32.930 --> 00:02:35.703
A canonical bridge, from Lesson five point

00:02:35.703 --> 00:02:38.840
one, is the safest, sometimes slower option.

00:02:38.840 --> 00:02:41.253
A fast bridge, or liquidity network, is

00:02:41.253 --> 00:02:44.510
quicker, relying on that network's own liquidity.

00:02:44.510 --> 00:02:47.998
An exchange deposit-and-withdraw is often the simplest

00:02:47.998 --> 00:02:50.915
choice for genuinely large amounts, but makes

00:02:50.915 --> 00:02:54.085
you briefly custodial, trusting that exchange for

00:02:54.085 --> 00:02:56.985
the moment your funds actually sit there.

00:02:56.985 --> 00:03:01.443
And intent-based cross-chain swaps let specialised solvers deliver your

00:03:01.443 --> 00:03:04.910
funds on the destination chain directly, on your behalf.

00:03:06.210 --> 00:03:10.087
Worth being precise about why an exchange specifically deserves

00:03:10.087 --> 00:03:13.115
its own separate mention here, since it's easy to

00:03:13.115 --> 00:03:15.535
overlook as a genuine route at all.

00:03:15.535 --> 00:03:17.947
For genuinely large amounts, it's often

00:03:17.947 --> 00:03:20.840
the simplest, most liquid option available.

00:03:20.840 --> 00:03:23.641
But for the specific window your funds sit on

00:03:23.641 --> 00:03:27.356
that exchange, you're trusting it directly, exactly the same

00:03:27.356 --> 00:03:31.192
custodial trust question covered back in earlier lessons, just

00:03:31.192 --> 00:03:33.750
for a shorter, deliberate stretch of time.

00:03:35.050 --> 00:03:38.456
Here's a practical way to actually choose between these

00:03:38.456 --> 00:03:42.045
four, based specifically on size, echoing the same pattern

00:03:42.045 --> 00:03:45.325
from Lesson five point one's own bridge decision.

00:03:45.325 --> 00:03:48.816
A small, routine amount reasonably fits a fast bridge

00:03:48.816 --> 00:03:52.371
or an intent-based swap, where speed matters more than

00:03:52.371 --> 00:03:55.515
squeezing out the very last bit of security.

00:03:55.515 --> 00:03:59.010
A genuinely large amount deserves the canonical route,

00:03:59.010 --> 00:04:02.949
or a carefully-checked exchange, specifically because size is

00:04:02.949 --> 00:04:05.935
exactly what's worth protecting most carefully.

00:04:07.235 --> 00:04:08.325
Quick check.

00:04:08.325 --> 00:04:11.390
What's the actual risk of chain abstraction, specifically?

00:04:15.810 --> 00:04:18.997
The answer: each layer it hides, smart accounts,

00:04:18.997 --> 00:04:22.964
paymasters, solvers, is still a real, additional dependency,

00:04:22.964 --> 00:04:25.435
even though it's now invisible to you.

00:04:26.735 --> 00:04:27.770
Chain abstraction.

00:04:29.070 --> 00:04:32.274
Here's exactly what chain abstraction actually does, and

00:04:32.274 --> 00:04:35.665
the specific mechanisms doing the hiding underneath it.

00:04:35.665 --> 00:04:39.585
Wallets and apps increasingly hide which chain you're actually

00:04:39.585 --> 00:04:42.633
on entirely, letting you pay gas in a stablecoin

00:04:42.633 --> 00:04:46.117
instead of that chain's own native token, or presenting

00:04:46.117 --> 00:04:50.088
one single balance across several different chains at once.

00:04:50.088 --> 00:04:53.305
Underneath all of that, smart accounts, paymasters,

00:04:53.305 --> 00:04:56.028
and solvers are the actual mechanisms doing

00:04:56.028 --> 00:04:58.193
this work, quietly, on your behalf.

00:04:59.492 --> 00:05:03.020
Worth being precise about the actual question worth asking,

00:05:03.020 --> 00:05:06.723
before relying on any chain-abstracted app, rather than simply

00:05:06.723 --> 00:05:09.963
enjoying the convenience without a second thought.

00:05:09.963 --> 00:05:13.967
Ask specifically what's actually happening underneath that simplified

00:05:13.967 --> 00:05:17.056
interface, which of these mechanisms is involved, and

00:05:17.056 --> 00:05:20.087
whether you'd genuinely be comfortable with each one

00:05:20.087 --> 00:05:22.775
individually, on its own merits, if it weren't

00:05:22.775 --> 00:05:24.148
hidden from view at all.

00:05:25.448 --> 00:05:28.796
Worth being completely direct about the one sentence

00:05:28.796 --> 00:05:32.081
this entire topic reduces to, since convenience and

00:05:32.081 --> 00:05:35.413
safety aren't automatically the same thing here.

00:05:35.413 --> 00:05:38.994
It's genuinely convenient, and every single layer that

00:05:38.994 --> 00:05:41.663
hides that complexity from you is also a

00:05:41.663 --> 00:05:44.983
real dependency, exactly like a bridge, an oracle,

00:05:44.983 --> 00:05:47.718
or an admin key, whether you can actually

00:05:47.718 --> 00:05:49.280
see it happening or not.

00:05:50.580 --> 00:05:51.585
Worked example.

00:05:52.885 --> 00:05:55.750
Here's the actual choice, laid out fully,

00:05:55.750 --> 00:05:59.025
for genuinely moving thirty thousand dollars of

00:05:59.025 --> 00:06:01.440
U.S.D.C. from Arbitrum to Base.

00:06:01.440 --> 00:06:05.472
A canonical route, through Ethereum itself, takes two separate

00:06:05.472 --> 00:06:09.420
transactions, is genuinely slower, and costs more in gas.

00:06:09.420 --> 00:06:12.697
A reputable fast bridge instead costs somewhere between

00:06:12.697 --> 00:06:15.564
five and fifteen dollars, and takes just minutes.

00:06:16.864 --> 00:06:20.181
Worth naming the third option too, an exchange, since

00:06:20.181 --> 00:06:23.614
it's a genuinely real choice for this exact amount.

00:06:23.614 --> 00:06:27.966
Withdraw directly on Base, specifically checking first that your

00:06:27.966 --> 00:06:32.185
exchange actually supports U.S.D.C. on both of those networks,

00:06:32.185 --> 00:06:35.399
before you ever deposit a single dollar into it.

00:06:36.699 --> 00:06:40.069
Here's exactly how to actually execute this, step by

00:06:40.069 --> 00:06:44.374
step, regardless of which specific route you ultimately choose.

00:06:44.374 --> 00:06:46.463
Send fifty dollars first, by your

00:06:46.463 --> 00:06:48.729
chosen route, as a genuine test.

00:06:48.729 --> 00:06:52.553
Keep roughly five dollars of E.T.H. on Base specifically

00:06:52.553 --> 00:06:55.939
for gas, before you send anything else at all.

00:06:55.939 --> 00:06:58.768
And only then send the remaining balance, once

00:06:58.768 --> 00:07:01.899
that small test has actually confirmed successfully.

00:07:03.199 --> 00:07:04.289
Quick check.

00:07:04.289 --> 00:07:06.564
What's genuinely the first step, on any

00:07:06.564 --> 00:07:09.124
new route, regardless of the amount involved?

00:07:13.544 --> 00:07:15.995
The answer: send a small test amount first,

00:07:15.995 --> 00:07:18.279
before committing the full balance to it.

00:07:19.579 --> 00:07:22.193
Worth being concrete about what recording the

00:07:22.193 --> 00:07:24.864
route actually looks like, in practice, rather

00:07:24.864 --> 00:07:27.039
than leaving it as a vague habit.

00:07:27.039 --> 00:07:29.800
A single line is genuinely enough: the date,

00:07:29.800 --> 00:07:32.623
the amount, the route you actually chose, and

00:07:32.623 --> 00:07:35.009
the fee and time it actually took.

00:07:35.009 --> 00:07:37.727
That real record is exactly what tells you,

00:07:37.727 --> 00:07:41.001
months later, which route genuinely worked well, and

00:07:41.001 --> 00:07:43.349
which one to actually avoid next time.

00:07:44.649 --> 00:07:46.109
Here's your checklist.

00:07:46.109 --> 00:07:49.103
Do it before moving real value between chains.

00:07:49.103 --> 00:07:51.186
Keep a genuine gas float on every

00:07:51.186 --> 00:07:53.753
chain you actually use, at all times.

00:07:53.753 --> 00:07:55.970
Choose your route by its actual trust

00:07:55.970 --> 00:07:58.420
model and the amount involved, never just

00:07:58.420 --> 00:08:00.528
by whichever one looks fastest.

00:08:00.528 --> 00:08:02.645
And send a real test amount first,

00:08:02.645 --> 00:08:05.366
every time, recording the route you actually

00:08:05.366 --> 00:08:06.878
used in your own journal.

00:08:08.178 --> 00:08:09.423
Let's recap.

00:08:09.423 --> 00:08:12.277
Gas stranding means holding tokens with no

00:08:12.277 --> 00:08:14.401
gas token to move them; a small

00:08:14.401 --> 00:08:17.488
float on every chain prevents it entirely.

00:08:17.488 --> 00:08:21.300
Four real routes exist: canonical, fast bridge, exchange,

00:08:21.300 --> 00:08:25.543
and intent-based, each with a genuinely different trade-off.

00:08:25.543 --> 00:08:29.058
Chain abstraction is genuinely convenient, but every

00:08:29.058 --> 00:08:32.572
layer it hides, smart accounts, paymasters, solvers,

00:08:32.572 --> 00:08:34.663
is still a real dependency.

00:08:34.663 --> 00:08:36.509
And on any real amount: a gas

00:08:36.509 --> 00:08:39.094
float first, a test transaction, then the

00:08:39.094 --> 00:08:41.248
rest, recorded in your own journal.

00:08:42.548 --> 00:08:46.048
Worth closing on how directly this lesson actually builds

00:08:46.048 --> 00:08:49.063
on two others already covered in this module.

00:08:49.063 --> 00:08:52.507
The route choice here is the exact same trust-model

00:08:52.507 --> 00:08:56.548
question from Lesson five point one's bridges, and operating

00:08:56.548 --> 00:08:59.197
on a new chain safely is the exact same

00:08:59.197 --> 00:09:03.039
habit from Lesson five point six's ecosystems, now simply

00:09:03.039 --> 00:09:05.953
applied together, in the same real decision.

00:09:07.253 --> 00:09:09.937
Keep a gas float everywhere, choose your route

00:09:09.937 --> 00:09:13.093
deliberately, and always send a test amount first.

00:09:13.093 --> 00:09:16.433
Next up, Lesson 5.8: reading smart-contract

00:09:16.433 --> 00:09:18.710
code, enough to verify claims.
